## Paper III

### Title

The $(3,4,\infty)$ family of complex six-spheres. III. Maps and the resulting manifolds

### Abstract

We compute the homotopy classes of three maps associated with the $(3,4,\infty)$ family of complex six-spheres. The torus quotient represents the element of order two in $\pi_6(S^3)$, the holomorphic map to $\mathbb P^1$ is null-homotopic, and the cusp map that keeps the angular coordinate represents the nonzero element of $\pi_6(S^4)$. Normal framings and integral attaching maps give these classes and a finite cell model. For every cyclic universal cover we calculate the integral cohomology ring and the complete smooth classification invariant. We classify the natural section-line sphere fillings over twenty-one elliptic profiles: there are $59$ marked patterns on $50$ initial data, with twelve profiles admitting unbounded multiplicity. We also determine weighted smooth types and the homology of every free OS49 affine cap, including its additional three-primary torsion. A weighted OS45 member has zero refined Pontryagin class, as follows from an embedded Hopf sphere and its critical-cycle linking number. The local Kummer cover has no smooth compact global extension at degree greater than one; an explicit finite flat normal extension has additional components above the cusp. Finally, moving the zero of the line-bundle linearization gives a conifold smoothing diffeomorphic to $S^3\times S^3$, of algebraic dimension one.

### Comments

Paper III of the five-paper series "The (3,4,∞) family of complex six-spheres". The main results determine the three sphere-map classes, compute integral and smooth invariants of the cyclic and elliptic constructions, and construct an algebraic-dimension-one conifold smoothing on S³×S³. The original complex six-sphere family is due to Claude and L. Alpöge (https://alpo.ge/s6.pdf). Principal construction inputs include Engel's elliptic-surface model (arXiv:2609.38442v1), Yang's toroidal fillings (arXiv:2609.29666v1), and Viaclovsky's two-parameter and logarithmic-transformation families (arXiv:2609.33785v1 and arXiv:2610.39102v1). Smooth recognition uses Zhubr's marked classification of simply connected spin six-manifolds and the oriented surgery theorem recorded by Burklund, Hahn and Senger (arXiv:1910.14116v3). Exact source statements and pages are given in the paper. The standing address for the records and programs is https://github.com/KokunoYumeto/s6-complex-structure-workbench/blob/main/RECORDS.md#paper-iii.

### Provenance

Text: AI-authored; none of it was written by a human. The submitter takes responsibility for the veracity of the text.

How it was produced. GPT-5.6 Sol (OpenAI), in Codex at reasoning effort ultra, produced the working edition on 24 August–9 September 2026. GPT-6 Astra (OpenAI), in ChatGPT Pro mode, carried out the research of 12–16 November 2026, independent re-derivations during 16–18 November, writing during 16–20 November, and publication work during 20–25 November. Its numerical reasoning-effort setting was not separately exposed. GPT-6 Astra Pro (OpenAI), in ChatGPT Pro mode, continued the publication work on 25 November 2026; a numerical reasoning-effort setting was not separately exposed. Claude Fable 5.2 (Anthropic), at effort high and at effort max, and Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), at effort max, performed readings, intake checks and recomputations during 12–20 November 2026. Claude Opus 5.5 at effort max also rewrote Paper V's abstract and introduction on 20 November, read all of Paper I on 21–22 November and adjusted its deposited text and links, read all of Paper II on 24 November, and checked publication material received during 23–25 November. These historical activities retain the specific scopes recorded for them.

How it was checked. The records associate 1,066 entries with the current statements of this paper: 421 examinations of proof steps, 2 independent re-derivations, 50 computations or formal certificates, 473 readings of proofs and applications, 90 uses of background results without a fault found, and 30 refutation records associated with their corrected statements. These entries belong to 799 recorded evidence groups. Each event is counted once; entries sharing an evidence group retain that common group. One argument or calculation can be associated with several statements and kinds of examination. Each outcome specifies its scope and prior proof exposure.

The models compared explicit statements, marked geometric data, proposed proofs and computations with the cited primary sources. The work includes derivations written before consulting earlier proofs, independent proofs, exact arithmetic and certified rational bounds. Geometric comparisons retain their coordinate maps, inverse maps, normal orientations and exceptional parameter values. Existing proofs were read in full against their replacements during publication work. These readings record use and rechecking of existing arguments; they do not by themselves add an independent derivation. Each recorded outcome states the passage or computation examined and its prior proof exposure. The rational Rouché certificates verify entire continuation segments, and the finite arithmetic checks have the mathematical purposes stated where they are used.

Human understanding. No human has checked the mathematics. The paper credits the literature at its supplying statements and pages, and states the exact domains of its own constructions and calculations.

Records. The standing address for Paper III's source editions, programs, outputs and check records is https://github.com/KokunoYumeto/s6-complex-structure-workbench/blob/main/RECORDS.md#paper-iii. The text, programs and check records are dedicated to the public domain under CC0 1.0 Universal (https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/).

